// @error is not on every member
// THE SINGLE-SURVIVOR BOUND, on the complement side, and it is LOUD rather than wrong.
//
// `u.k == "x"` being false leaves TWO arms of a three-arm union. The EMITTER's narrowing
// stack takes a variant NAME, so a two-arm survivor set would need an interned member-SET id
// instead — which this rule does not mint — and it therefore pushes nothing and keeps the
// full union.
//
// THE CHECKER IS THE MORE PRECISE OF THE TWO HERE, and the message shows it: it narrows to
// `A | B` and rejects the read against THAT, which is both true and more informative than
// rejecting against `U`. The emitter keeps the full union. That asymmetry costs nothing
// silent — a field the checker would allow on the two survivors is one the emitter's
// shared-field plan then refuses for want of the third arm, loudly — and both layers relax
// together the day the narrowing stack learns a member-SET id.
//
// A field present on ALL THREE arms still reads here — that goes through the shared-field
// plan and needs no narrowing at all (`shared-field-read-reps.vl`).
type A = { k: "a", n: f64 }
type B = { k: "b", s: f64 }
type X = { k: "x", m: f64 }
type U = A | B | X

function f(u: U) {
  if u.k == "x" { return u.m } else { return u.n }
}
print(f({ k: "x", m: 5.0 }))
